Introdução
A Programação Orientada a Objetos nasceu com a linguagem Simula 67, considerada a primeira linguagem orientada a objetos, e foi consolidada e popularizada por Smalltalk nos anos 1970. Ganhou adoção massiva na indústria com C++ e, posteriormente, Java e C#. A ideia central é organizar o código em torno de objetos: unidades que combinam dados (atributos/estado) e comportamento (métodos) em uma única estrutura.
Os quatro pilares
- Encapsulamento — os dados internos de um objeto são protegidos e só podem ser acessados/alterados através de métodos expostos, escondendo detalhes de implementação do mundo externo.
- 
Abstração — o objeto expõe apenas o que é relevante para quem o utiliza, escondendo a complexidade interna (ex.: você chama CalcularSalario() sem precisar saber como o cálculo é feito por dentro).
- 
Herança — uma classe pode herdar atributos e métodos de outra, permitindo reaproveitamento e especialização (uma classe Desenvolvedor pode herdar deFuncionario ).
- 
Polimorfismo — objetos de classes diferentes podem responder de forma diferente ao mesmo "chamado" (o mesmo método CalcularSalario() se comporta de forma distinta para umDesenvolvedor e para umGerente , por exemplo).
Exemplo
// Exemplo de Programação Orientada a Objetos em C#
public abstract class Funcionario
{
    public string Nome { get; }
    protected decimal SalarioBase { get; }
    protected Funcionario(string nome, decimal salarioBase)
    {
        Nome = nome;
        SalarioBase = salarioBase;
    }
    // Abstração: cada subclasse decide como calcular seu próprio salário
    public abstract decimal CalcularSalario();
}
public class Desenvolvedor : Funcionario
{
    private int BonusPorProjeto { get; }
    public Desenvolvedor(string nome, decimal salarioBase, int bonusPorProjeto)
        : base(nome, salarioBase) // Herança
    {
        BonusPorProjeto = bonusPorProjeto;
    }
    // Polimorfismo: implementação específica do método herdado
    public override decimal CalcularSalario()
    {
        return SalarioBase + BonusPorProjeto;
    }
}
public class Gerente : Funcionario
{
    private decimal BonusPorEquipe { get; }
    public Gerente(string nome, decimal salarioBase, decimal bonusPorEquipe)
        : base(nome, salarioBase)
    {
        BonusPorEquipe = bonusPorEquipe;
    }
    public override decimal CalcularSalario()
    {
        return SalarioBase + BonusPorEquipe;
    }
}
Aqui, SalarioBase é protegido (encapsulamento), Desenvolvedor e Gerente reaproveitam a estrutura de Funcionario (herança), e cada um implementa CalcularSalario() à sua maneira (polimorfismo) — tudo isso sem que o código que consome Funcionario precise saber qual subtipo específico está manipulando.
Vantagens e limitações
- Vantagens: modela bem domínios do mundo real com entidades ricas e relacionamentos complexos; favorece reuso e organização em sistemas grandes; se encaixa naturalmente em arquiteturas como Clean Architecture e DDD (Domain-Driven Design).
- Limitações: hierarquias de herança profundas podem se tornar rígidas e difíceis de alterar; o estado mutável encapsulado em objetos ainda pode gerar efeitos colaterais difíceis de rastrear se mal gerenciado.
Linguagens representativas: Java, C#, Python, C++, TypeScript (com classes).

I forked the DataStax Cassandra driver when it stopped compiling on PHP 8 and most of its maintainers had already moved on. My first instinct was to write the new parts in C++. I built a Zend wrapper class, used RAII throughout, and put smart pointers around zvals—the whole modern setup.
It introduced memory bugs that took me days to track down, and I did not get a meaningful benefit in return.
So the driver is being rewritten in C23.
I want to explain why, because “just use C++; it’s safer” is the reflexive answer. For a PHP extension, I no longer think it is the right one.
This is not an argument that C++ is a bad language. In an application where I own the allocator, error model, and object lifetimes, std::vector and std::unique_ptr earn their keep. A PHP extension is different: the Zend Engine owns those rules, and its rules are written in C.
The problem is not that C++ cannot call the Zend API. Plenty of extensions do. The problem is impedance: each abstraction has to be taught PHP’s lifetime rules, and the teaching code can become more complicated than the work it was meant to simplify.
These are the four places where that cost me real debugging time.
PHP owns the allocator
PHP has its own memory manager. Request-scoped memory is allocated with functions such as emalloc, ecalloc, and safe_emalloc, then released with efree. Zend tracks that memory and normally reclaims what remains at request shutdown. Persistent allocations use a separate API because they have a different lifetime.
Plain malloc and free—and therefore ordinary new and delete—sit outside that request-memory model. The moment I put a std::vector<zval> in an extension, its backing storage uses the C++ allocator unless I replace it.
The obvious fix is a custom allocator:
template <class T>
struct PhpAllocator {
    using value_type = T;
    template <class U>
    PhpAllocator(const PhpAllocator<U>&) noexcept {}
    PhpAllocator() noexcept = default;
    [[nodiscard]] T* allocate(std::size_t n) {
        return static_cast<T*>(safe_emalloc(n, sizeof(T), 0));
    }
    void deallocate(T* p, std::size_t) noexcept {
        efree(p);
    }
};
std::vector<zval, PhpAllocator<zval>> values;
That fixes where the buffer is allocated. It does not reconcile the failure models.
A standard C++ allocation path reports failure through an exception such as std::bad_alloc. Zend can instead abort the request with zend_bailout, implemented with setjmp/longjmp. If that jump crosses an automatic C++ object with a non-trivial destructor, the C++ standard says the behavior is undefined. In practical terms, destructors are not a cleanup mechanism I can rely on across a Zend bailout.
The container and the engine each follow their own contract. The adapter between them does not make the contracts compatible.
The C version does not pretend otherwise:
zval *values = safe_emalloc(count, sizeof(zval), 0);
/* use values */
efree(values);
If allocation bails out, it bails out. There is no C++ stack-unwinding contract in the middle of the operation.
Most of the time I do not need a standard container anyway. PHP already gives me HashTable and the zend_hash_* API, the same machinery the engine uses for arrays. I am not reaching for std::unordered_map when the engine already provides the data structure that integrates with its allocator and value model.
The one container I genuinely miss is std::vector. For this driver, that was not enough reason to introduce another runtime dependency and another lifetime abstraction around one growable array.


  A zval has no C++ destructor
This was the bug that cost me days.
A zval is a C struct. On a 64-bit PHP build it is typically 16 bytes. Scalars such as integers, doubles, booleans, and null live directly in it. Strings, arrays, objects, resources, and references involve refcounted payloads. Releasing such a value requires Zend’s destruction logic, normally through zval_ptr_dtor.
std::vector<zval> does not know any of that. To C++, zval is a trivially destructible value. Destroying the vector frees its buffer but does not release the payload owned by each element.
std::vector<zval> row;
row.push_back(*val); // bitwise copy; no refcount increment
That line contains two bugs.
First, destroying row will not call zval_ptr_dtor for its elements, so refcounted payloads leak. Second, the implicit copy duplicates the payload pointer without incrementing its refcount. Two zvals can now point to the same payload while Zend still sees a refcount of one. Destroy one and the other becomes a dangling reference.
The next obvious fix is a wrapper:
struct Zval {
    zval value;
    Zval() { ZVAL_UNDEF(&value); }
    ~Zval() { zval_ptr_dtor(&value); }
};
It looks finished. It is not.
The compiler-generated copy constructor still performs the wrong bitwise copy. A correct wrapper needs explicit copy construction, move construction, copy assignment, and move assignment. Copying must use Zend’s refcount-aware operations. Moving must leave the source as IS_UNDEF so its destructor becomes a no-op. Assignment must release the old destination before replacing it.
In other words, I have to reproduce Zend’s value semantics inside a C++ class and keep that class correct across every PHP version the driver supports.
The C code is longer, but its ownership operations are visible:
zval *row = safe_emalloc(count, sizeof(zval), 0);
for (uint32_t i = 0; i < count; i++) {
    ZVAL_COPY(&row[i], &src[i]);
}
/* use row */
for (uint32_t i = 0; i < count; i++) {
    zval_ptr_dtor(&row[i]);
}
efree(row);
There is more ceremony, yes. But the refcount increments and decrements are written exactly where they happen. I do not have an implicit special member function deciding Zend ownership behind my back.
For this codebase, explicit was safer.


  unique_ptr fixes ownership by changing the layout
The next suggestion is a smart pointer. A zval needs special destruction, and std::unique_ptr supports a custom deleter:
struct ZvalDeleter {
    void operator()(zval* z) const noexcept {
        zval_ptr_dtor(z);
        efree(z);
    }
};
using ZvalPtr = std::unique_ptr<zval, ZvalDeleter>;
std::vector<ZvalPtr> row;
That can make ownership explicit, but it changes the representation.
A zval is designed to be stored inline. With unique_ptr, every element instead needs its own allocation. The vector holds pointers; each pointer leads to a separately allocated zval; and a refcounted zval then points to its payload.
For a result row with n columns, an inline buffer needs one allocation. The pointer version needs one allocation for the vector buffer plus up to n allocations for the individual values. Scalars that used to live directly in contiguous storage now require a pointer chase.
That matters in a database driver. A large part of its hot path is turning database values into PHP values, row after row. Adding an allocation per column is not an abstract cost; it is exactly the kind of cost that compounds there.
There is also a semantic mismatch. A unique_ptr expresses unique ownership of an object. A zval participates in Zend’s copy-on-write and refcounting rules. The smart pointer can own the zval allocation, but it does not model the ownership of the value represented by that zval.
The C layout is the one Zend expects: one contiguous allocation, scalars inline, and explicit destruction for the values that need it.
RAII and Zend bailouts do not compose
This is the issue that made me stop relying on exceptions and RAII for request cleanup.
Normal PHP exceptions and Zend bailouts are not the same thing. Calling zend_throw_exception creates a PHP exception object and records it in the engine; extension code is expected to return and propagate failure in the usual Zend way. A bailout is the harder escape path used for fatal conditions such as an unrecoverable allocation failure.
A C++ exception unwinds the stack and runs destructors. A Zend bailout jumps to an engine-managed recovery point. It does not perform C++ stack unwinding, and crossing non-trivial automatic C++ objects is outside the behavior the C++ standard defines.
That distinction matters because RAII is most valuable on failure paths. If a bailout can cross a scope, the destructors in that scope cannot be the only mechanism protecting Zend values, external resources, locks, or other state that request shutdown cannot repair for me.
PHP’s memory manager will normally reclaim leaked request allocations at the end of the request, but it does not run my C++ destructors. Reclaiming a block of memory is not the same as releasing a Cassandra handle, decrementing a Zend refcount at the correct time, or undoing partially updated external state.
This does not mean every Zend API call bails out. Many report failure, set EG(exception), or expect the extension to return. The engineering problem is that the bailout boundary still exists, and generic C++ code is not designed around it.
The driver therefore uses the engine’s own error model: PHP exceptions where appropriate, explicit return-code checks, and cleanup that does not assume C++ stack unwinding will always occur.
The small mismatches accumulate
None of these issues alone forced the rewrite. The accumulated friction did.
The shared-library entry point must keep C linkage so PHP can find an unmangled get_module symbol. Version-specific differences in Zend APIs still need preprocessor guards. A wrapper layer can move those guards elsewhere, but it cannot remove the ABI differences they represent.
Standard-library facilities also bring trade-offs that matter more in extension code than they do in an application: possible allocations, indirect calls, larger binaries, and a C++ runtime dependency for the shared object. Some of those costs can be optimized away. Some are negligible outside a hot path. The problem was not any single feature; it was that I kept adding machinery to recover the layout and behavior Zend already exposed directly in C.
What I measured
I wanted to know whether this was merely a maintenance preference, so I built a small loadable extension with four implementations of the same operation. Each implementation builds and destroys a row of n string zvals. The source strings are reused, so string creation is outside the timed region.
The test ran on PHP 8.5.7 on an Apple M4 Pro. The C code was compiled as C23 and the C++ code as C++23, both with -O3 -march=native. The reported time is the minimum of seven repeats.
For a row 256 columns wide, I measured:
| Strategy | ns/row | Allocations requested/row | Payload bytes requested/row | vs. C | 
|---|---|---|---|---|
| C, raw emalloc buffer | 659 | 1 | 4,096 | 1.00× | 
| C++ PhpVector<Zval> | 1,214 | 1 | 4,096 | 1.84× | 
| C++ PhpVector<ZvalUniquePtr> | 1,969 | 257 | 6,144 | 2.99× | 
| C++ hand-rolled ZvalRow | 710 | 1 | 4,096 | 1.08× | 
“Payload bytes” means bytes requested by these benchmark allocations, not the allocator’s total physical footprint. It excludes allocator metadata, size-class rounding, and the reused source strings. The table is useful for comparing the shapes of these implementations, not for claiming their exact resident-memory cost.
The allocation count explains most of the pointer version’s behavior. The C and inline-container versions request one row allocation regardless of width. The unique_ptr version requests one for the pointer buffer and one for every element. At 256 columns that is 257 allocations for one row.
The inline C++ vector uses the same number of row allocations as C, but this implementation still measured 1.84 times slower. Its hot loop constructs a wrapper, moves it into the vector, and maintains the wrapper’s lifetime invariants. That result describes this wrapper and this loop; it is not proof that every std::vector operation is intrinsically 1.84 times slower than C.
The hand-rolled ZvalRow got much closer. It is a move-only, single-purpose container over one Zend allocation. The safe version zero-initializes its buffer so any slots not filled before ordinary scope exit are valid IS_UNDEF values:
template <bool Refcounted>
class ZvalRow {
    std::size_t size_;
    zval* data_;
public:
    explicit ZvalRow(std::size_t size) noexcept
        : size_(size),
          data_(static_cast<zval*>(ecalloc(size, sizeof(zval)))) {}
    ZvalRow(const ZvalRow&) = delete;
    ~ZvalRow() {
        if constexpr (Refcounted) {
            for (std::size_t i = 0; i < size_; i++) {
                zval_ptr_dtor(&data_[i]);
            }
        }
        efree(data_);
    }
    zval* data() noexcept { return data_; }
};
There are other safe designs. The container could track how many elements have been initialized instead of zeroing the entire buffer. That would trade the zero-fill for bookkeeping in the construction loop. I measured the zero-initialized version because it kept the timed fill loop closest to the C loop.
With plain emalloc, the hand-rolled version matched C in my test, but its destructor assumed every slot contained a valid zval. An ordinary early return or C++ exception during construction could make it walk uninitialized storage. Switching to ecalloc removed that bug and produced the 1.08× result above. A Zend bailout is a separate case: it skips the destructor rather than invoking it early.
I swept the row width from 4 to 16,384 columns. The exact ratios moved, but the allocation shape did not. One allocation remained one; the pointer version grew as n + 1. At 16,384 columns it requested 16,385 allocations and 384 KiB of payload storage, compared with one allocation and 256 KiB for the inline C representation on this 64-bit build.
The conclusion I draw is narrower than “C++ is slow.” C++ can get close when I replace the generic abstractions with a purpose-built container that mirrors the Zend representation. But at that point I have written another Zend-specific lifetime layer, and I still have to account for bailouts explicitly.
For this driver, C gets me to the native representation without the detour.
The core benchmark loops
The C path is one allocation followed by explicit copy and destruction loops:
void bench_c_row(long n, long iterations, zend_string **src) {
    for (long iteration = 0; iteration < iterations; iteration++) {
        zval *row = safe_emalloc((size_t) n, sizeof(zval), 0);
        for (long i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            ZVAL_STR_COPY(&row[i], src[i]);
        }
        for (long i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            zval_ptr_dtor(&row[i]);
        }
        efree(row);
    }
}
The inline wrapper needs all five special member functions so copies and moves preserve Zend’s ownership rules:
struct Zval {
    zval value;
    Zval() { ZVAL_UNDEF(&value); }
    ~Zval() { zval_ptr_dtor(&value); }
    Zval(const Zval& other) {
        ZVAL_COPY(&value, const_cast<zval*>(&other.value));
    }
    Zval(Zval&& other) noexcept {
        ZVAL_COPY_VALUE(&value, &other.value);
        ZVAL_UNDEF(&other.value);
    }
    Zval& operator=(const Zval& other) {
        if (this != &other) {
            zval_ptr_dtor(&value);
            ZVAL_COPY(&value, const_cast<zval*>(&other.value));
        }
        return *this;
    }
    Zval& operator=(Zval&& other) noexcept {
        if (this != &other) {
            zval_ptr_dtor(&value);
            ZVAL_COPY_VALUE(&value, &other.value);
            ZVAL_UNDEF(&other.value);
        }
        return *this;
    }
};
The vector loop then looks like this:
std::vector<Zval, PhpAllocator<Zval>> row;
row.reserve((std::size_t) n);
for (long i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    Zval value;
    ZVAL_STR_COPY(&value.value, src[i]);
    row.push_back(std::move(value));
}
One important detail: ZVAL_STR_COPY initializes a fresh destination and increments the string’s refcount, but it does not destroy a value already stored there. In these loops the destination starts as IS_UNDEF. Reusing an occupied destination requires releasing its previous value first.
The module entry point needs C linkage when the module is compiled as C++:
extern "C" {
    ZEND_GET_MODULE(cvscpp)
}
Without that linkage, the exported get_module name may be mangled and PHP will fail to load the extension.
The boundary of the claim
This benchmark covers one operation: building result rows of zvals. That happens to be central to a database driver, but it is not the whole Zend API.
PHP provides HashTable for arrays, object handlers for objects, and persistent allocation functions for data that must outlive a request. Each has its own lifetime rules. I expect similar integration costs when wrapping them in generic C++ abstractions, but I did not benchmark them, so I am not attaching numbers to them.
The exact timings above belong to one machine, one PHP build, one allocator, one compiler, and one payload type. The structural result is more portable than the multipliers: an inline row requests one allocation, while the per-element unique_ptr representation requests n + 1.
Nor does C give me memory safety. It has the same sharp edges it has always had. My claim is only that, inside someone else’s C engine, I have fewer competing lifetime rules to hold in my head when I use the engine’s native representation directly.
If I were starting another PHP extension today, I would write it in C, use Zend’s containers, make ownership operations explicit, and check every return path. It is less clever, and in this driver there is much less to go wrong.
I already spent the clever version’s debugging budget.
